1)  What is Natural Language Processing?

Imagine trying to teach a computer to read, understand, and reply in plain human language — not code, not symbols, just the same everyday words you use with a friend. That, at its core, is what Natural Language Processing (NLP) is all about.

NLP is a branch of Artificial Intelligence that sits at the crossroads of computer science, linguistics, and statistics. Its goal is to give machines the ability to interpret, process, and generate human language in a way that feels natural and meaningful. Language is one of the most complex things humans produce — full of slang, sarcasm, context, and cultural nuance — which makes teaching it to a computer a genuinely hard problem.
Think about all the things you do with language every day: you ask questions, read news, translate phrases, dictate voice messages, or get product recommendations. Behind each of these lies an NLP system quietly doing the heavy lifting.
At a technical level, NLP models learn statistical patterns from enormous amounts of text data. When you type a sentence, the model doesn't "understand" it the way a human does — instead, it maps words to numerical vectors and finds mathematical relationships between them, which mimics understanding well enough to be genuinely useful.

2)  Steps Involved in Solving an NLP Problem
    Solving any NLP problem is not a single magical step — it's a structured pipeline, almost like a recipe. Each stage transforms raw, messy text into something a model can reason about. Here's how that journey unfolds:
STEP 01
Data Collection
Gather raw text — articles, tweets, emails, reviews — relevant to your task.
STEP 02
Text Preprocessing
Clean the text: lowercase it, remove noise, strip HTML and special characters.
STEP 03
Tokenization
Split text into tokens — usually words or subwords — as the basic units.
STEP 04
Stopword Removal
Remove common words like "the", "is", "and" that carry little meaning.
STEP 05
Stemming / Lemmatization
Reduce words to their root form. "running" → "run", "better" → "good".
STEP 06
Feature Extraction
Convert text to numbers — TF-IDF, word embeddings (Word2Vec, BERT).
STEP 07
Model Training
Train a model (Naive Bayes, LSTM, Transformer) on labelled examples.
STEP 08
Evaluation
Measure performance with accuracy, F1-score, precision, and recall.
STEP 09
Deployment
Integrate the model into an app or API for real-world use.
The most time-consuming step in practice is almost always preprocessing. Real-world text is messy — full of typos, emojis, inconsistent formatting, and multiple languages. A well-prepared dataset is often the difference between a mediocre model and a great one. As the saying goes in data science: garbage in, garbage out.


3) Ensemble Methods in NLP
  Here's a thought experiment: if you were unsure about an important decision, would you rely on the opinion of just one expert, or would you ask several experts and go with the majority view? Most people would choose the latter — and that's exactly the intuition behind ensemble methods.

In NLP, an ensemble method combines the predictions of multiple models to produce a final result that is more accurate and robust than any single model alone. Different models have different strengths and weaknesses — one might be great at catching patterns in long sentences while another excels at short, punchy text. By combining them, you compensate for each model's blind spots.

There are three main flavours of ensemble methods you'll encounter in NLP:

Voting: Each model casts a "vote" and the majority wins, as shown above. Simple but surprisingly effective.

Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating): Multiple versions of the same model are trained on different random subsets of data. Random Forest is the classic example — it builds many decision trees and averages their results.

Stacking: The outputs of several base models are fed as inputs into a higher-level "meta-model" that learns the best way to combine them. This is the most powerful but also the most complex approach.

Ensemble methods are especially popular in NLP competitions (like Kaggle) where squeezing out the last few percentage points of accuracy matters. The trade-off is higher computational cost and more complexity to maintain — but for production systems where mistakes are expensive, the accuracy gains are well worth it.
